"1. Load the three raw datasets from the working directory files `u.data`, `VideoGames.csv`, and `user_taggedartists-timestamps.dat` using LensKit 0.14.4-compatible I/O.\n2. Apply 5-core filtering to each dataset before any splitting; repeat filtering until all users and items meet the 5-core threshold.\n3. Convert MovieLens100K and Amazon Video Games to implicit feedback by retaining only ratings strictly greater than 3 and treating each retained event as an interaction.\n4. For Last.FM, construct implicit interactions directly from the user-tagged-artists file without applying the >3 rating filter, while still enforcing 5-core filtering.\n5. Standardize each dataset into the LensKit implicit-feedback event format required by the algorithms and evaluation pipeline.\n6. Generate exactly 5 distinct random seeds and use each seed to create an independent user-based 80/20 holdout split.\n7. Ensure the split is user-based so every test interaction belongs to a user with training history, and prevent leakage between train and test within each seed.\n8. Train and evaluate three algorithms only: ALS, ItemKNN, and Pop, using LensKit 0.14.4 implementations.\n9. Use standard/default hyperparameters for each algorithm; do not tune on the test set or customize parameters unless required by LensKit defaults.\n10. For each dataset, seed, and algorithm, fit on the training split and produce ranked recommendation lists for the held-out users.\n11. Compute Precision@k and nDCG@k for k = 1, 5, and 10 using the same evaluation definition across all runs.\n12. Aggregate results across the 5 seeds for each dataset-algorithm-metric-k combination and report summary statistics suitable for comparing seed sensitivity.\n13. Perform a short statistical analysis of seed effects, such as mean/standard deviation across seeds and a simple significance or variability assessment.\n14. Make the experiment fully reproducible by fixing and logging all seeds, preprocessing choices, split strategy, algorithm settings, and metric definitions.\n15. Save or print a concise results table containing dataset, algorithm, seed, metric, k, and score so the analysis can be verified and reproduced."